{:edb/db-before-t 863 :edb/db-after-t 864 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534176 50 #inst "2026-10-06T07:41:53.319Z" 13194139534176 true] [17592186063961 1100 "Which of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted below?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=2$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=2$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=2$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/0811362e235ec7c6e93a8bf892a451f8c347d0f633f251b9ed955062a53dd6c3.png)" 13194139534176 true] [17592186063961 1100 "Which of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted below?\n\n1. $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=2$\n2. $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=2$\n3. $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=2$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/313/Source/Images/8f7d963291fa66ef93140a470b918711.png)" 13194139534176 false] [17592186063961 1145 "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Stationary points are points where $f'(x) = 0$. In this case, the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=2$ and therefore, $f'(2)=0$.\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=2$ since it is the highest point of a peak in a graph.\n- Statement III is true. The function has a critical point at $x=2$ since each stationary point is a critical point.\n\nTherefore, all three statements are true." 13194139534176 false] [17592186063961 1145 "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Stationary points are points where $f'(x) = 0.$ In this case, the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=2$ and therefore, $f'(2)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=2$ since it is the highest point of a peak in a graph.\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has a critical point at $x=2$ since each stationary point is a critical point.\n\nTherefore, all three statements are true." 13194139534176 true] [17592186064029 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/50581635273c185b9ca3aefa907b2e2a4981ec1bf6be1c687bec47591e126a89.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. What are the $x$-coordinates of the stationary points?" 13194139534176 true] [17592186064029 1100 "The graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. What are the $x$-coordinates of the stationary points?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/313/Source/Images/990ea49ffef9a8e63c9c7297b519a4be.png)" 13194139534176 false] [17592186064029 1145 "A stationary point of $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ where $f'(x)=0$. Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that at $x=0$ and $x=4$, the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have $f'(x)=0$ at those points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/313/Source/Images/14acd809b743af3aa09ceab5ab003019.png)\n\nSo, there are two stationary points, and their $x$-coordinates are $x=0$ and $x=4$.\n\n**Note:** At $x=2$, there is a sharp corner, so $f'(2)$ doesn't exist. This gives one more critical point. But this is not a stationary point." 13194139534176 false] [17592186064029 1145 "A stationary point of $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ where $f'(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.  \n\nIn the given graph, we can see that at $x=0$ and $x=4,$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have $f'(x)=0$ at those points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/56e798c0942ddcf0b1786afb054ab6dfbea7768c86751555808d86cc4cfa89f6.png)\n\n  \n\nSo, there are two stationary points, and their $x$-coordinates are $x=0$ and $x=4$.\n\n**Note:** At $x=2$, there is a sharp corner, so $f'(2)$ doesn't exist. This gives one more critical point. But this is not a stationary point." 13194139534176 true] [17592186064078 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280587 13194139534176 true] [17592186064078 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280594 13194139534176 true] [17592186064078 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280601 13194139534176 true] [17592186064078 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280608 13194139534176 true] [17592186064185 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280573 13194139534176 true] [17592186064185 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280580 13194139534176 true] [17592186064185 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280615 13194139534176 true] [17592186064185 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280622 13194139534176 true] [17592186064185 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280629 13194139534176 true] [17592186064751 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534176 true] [17592186064751 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/bb17384f2313820f526f1ba4356f6f3c9e11fbb3791e4f7e2c6044cc604bf662.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=2$" 13194139534176 true] [17592186064751 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/313/Source/Images/q-48689.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y = f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n1. $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x = 2$\n2. $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x = 2$\n3. $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x = 2$" 13194139534176 false] [17592186064751 1145 "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ In this case, the graph of $f(x)$ has a has a sharp edge (cusp) at $x=2.$  Therefore, ${f}^{′}(2)$ does not exist and hence it can't be equal to zero.\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=2$ since $f(x)\\le f(2)$ for $x$ near the point $x=2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has a critical point (a corner) at $x=2$ since the derivative does not exist at this point." 13194139534176 true] [17592186280573 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280574 13194139534176 true] [17592186280573 1089 "q-103432" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280573 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534176 true] [17592186280573 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/55d089ab7c7947e3f9d67a11fd69fa3903abd6f10ef09853fe018eaa16524ec1.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above. What are the $x$-coordinates of all the stationary points?" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280573 1113 #uuid "399a7648-b41d-51df-9f7e-45ef9ac8e709" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280573 1145 "The stationary points of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that\n\n- at any point between $x=-5$ and $x=-2$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at those points,\n\n- at any point between $x=2$ and $x=5$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at those points,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/6983f3615bf721529173c11de3a9218962a57e4be5247f4bda1ac2fa496531ee.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinates of the stationary points of $f(x)$ are all values in the intervals $(-5,-2)$ and $(2,5).$ \n\n**Note:** At $x=-2$ and $x=2$, there are sharp corners, so ${f}^{′}(x)$ doesn't exist at those points. This gives us two more critical points, but they are not stationary points." 13194139534176 true] [17592186280574 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280575 13194139534176 true] [17592186280574 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280576 13194139534176 true] [17592186280574 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280577 13194139534176 true] [17592186280574 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280578 13194139534176 true] [17592186280574 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280579 13194139534176 true] [17592186280574 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534176 true] [17592186280574 1041 "selection" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280574 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280579 13194139534176 true] [17592186280574 1110 #uuid "a5aa1f45-10ca-521e-9aff-a2310eeeead9" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280575 1034 "$x=-2$ and $x=2$ only" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280575 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280575 1111 #uuid "19509a20-4416-55b8-985a-d2da1754bcc4" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280576 1034 "All points in the closed interval $[-2,2]$" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280576 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280576 1111 #uuid "56caf002-ef8f-5f09-a5b4-d1138de10f47" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280577 1034 "All points in the open interval $(-2,2)$" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280577 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280577 1111 #uuid "5093fe11-129e-5e5a-918a-c8885801b1ba" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280578 1034 "All points in the closed intervals $(-5,-2]$ and $[2,5)$" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280578 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280578 1111 #uuid "8c3de4b7-7d09-56a4-80a5-4dd4b5b6e3b7" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280579 1034 "All points in the intervals $(-5,-2)$ and $(2,5)$" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280579 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280579 1111 #uuid "cb112ede-bd16-5541-9627-5700d8f98df2" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280580 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280581 13194139534176 true] [17592186280580 1089 "q-103434" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280580 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534176 true] [17592186280580 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/07c617933910b3d82ed397fd77754beb2b49fef9bfb498dea3f51a392f6c1d03.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above. What are the $x$-coordinates of all the stationary points?" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280580 1113 #uuid "7d36563a-6e2c-5e9e-9cc2-a7be86e291cf" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280580 1145 "The stationary points of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that\n\n- at $x=-3$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{′}(-3)=0,$\n\n- at $x=-1$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{′}(-1)=0,$\n\n- at any point between $x=2$ and $x=4$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at those points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/d8a2a08999b45a256fc0458069e50771c2c4ee03ab4f2ed1166f3aa8a1c70c83.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinates of the stationary points of $f(x)$ are $x=-3,$ $x=-1,$ and all values in the open interval $(2,4).$\n\n**Note:** At $x=0$ and $x=2$ there are sharp corners, so ${f}^{′}(x)$ doesn't exist at those points. This gives us two more critical points, but they are not stationary points." 13194139534176 true] [17592186280581 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280582 13194139534176 true] [17592186280581 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280583 13194139534176 true] [17592186280581 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280584 13194139534176 true] [17592186280581 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280585 13194139534176 true] [17592186280581 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280586 13194139534176 true] [17592186280581 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534176 true] [17592186280581 1041 "selection" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280581 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280584 13194139534176 true] [17592186280581 1110 #uuid "5124d7b7-38e7-515e-a83e-0f09948badb4" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280582 1034 "x\\in {-1,0}\\cup [2,4)" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280582 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534176 true] [17592186280582 1111 #uuid "692bcfab-a633-5b18-9ce0-ddabf9fc5e01" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280583 1034 "x\\in {-3,-1}" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280583 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534176 true] [17592186280583 1111 #uuid "820a4ea4-e49a-52db-872f-67598e3abb3f" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280584 1034 "x\\in {-3,-1}\\cup (2,4)" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280584 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534176 true] [17592186280584 1111 #uuid "161b45b3-450d-5498-b630-360399b890ba" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280585 1034 "x\\in (2,4)" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280585 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534176 true] [17592186280585 1111 #uuid "d9f89571-144c-55ea-bf8a-4e07b6f1cc38" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280586 1034 "x\\in {-3,-1,0,2}" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280586 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534176 true] [17592186280586 1111 #uuid "e03e9ab1-4602-5166-aa1e-5d30756fe5af" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280587 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280588 13194139534176 true] [17592186280587 1089 "q-103442" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280587 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534176 true] [17592186280587 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/7dc7480c7ee27073ff7be7475161742e317b30bf0c3e35af33dc93a772e6034a.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=-1$\n- $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=1$\n- $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=3$" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280587 1113 #uuid "bc25f65f-6157-58dc-8973-bfb8847f3624" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280587 1145 "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function has a critical point (a corner) at $x=-1.$ In this case,  ${f}^{′}(-1)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement II is false. The function has a critical point (a corner) at $x=1.$ In this case,  ${f}^{′}(-1)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement III is false. The function has a local minimum at $x=3$ since $f(3)\\le f(x)$ for all $x$ close enough to $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true, while statements II and III are false." 13194139534176 true] [17592186280588 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280589 13194139534176 true] [17592186280588 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280590 13194139534176 true] [17592186280588 1