e global maximum of $f(x)?$" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280594 1113 #uuid "62b39803-cf8b-5f2a-91f9-b83f48f12d0e" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280594 1145 "The global extrema of a function are the lowest or highest points of the function's graph over its entire domain.\n\nThe lowest point is the global minimum, whereas the highest point is the global maximum.\n\nAccording to the graph, the highest points are $B$ and $D$, and so these points represents the global maximum of $f(x).$" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280595 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280596 13194139534176 true] [17592186280595 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280597 13194139534176 true] [17592186280595 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280598 13194139534176 true] [17592186280595 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280599 13194139534176 true] [17592186280595 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280600 13194139534176 true] [17592186280595 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534176 true] [17592186280595 1041 "selection" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280595 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280597 13194139534176 true] [17592186280595 1110 #uuid "771a0a25-0693-5931-a5ce-28de845dbde3" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280596 1034 "$A$ and $C$ only" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280596 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280596 1111 #uuid "90a80798-1acb-5c8c-9c73-003aeeca8168" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280597 1034 "$B$ and $D$ only" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280597 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280597 1111 #uuid "e7f7dc24-7f82-5eae-ab2f-0810d3845890" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280598 1034 "$B$ and $C$ only" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280598 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280598 1111 #uuid "709e56e6-2610-5af0-a991-a0856ce8ac5f" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280599 1034 "$C$ only" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280599 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280599 1111 #uuid "8d31cb47-0e46-5186-8664-e1c5c23d3e53" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280600 1034 "$B$ only" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280600 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280600 1111 #uuid "3c563599-2a2f-54e7-a9ec-1f2eda3b11b0" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280601 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280602 13194139534176 true] [17592186280601 1089 "q-48734" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280601 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534176 true] [17592186280601 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/14fdc374842064ad40ebbadf3dab16191ecfdeec8c416b4e7f17beffce44fb40.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=3$\n- $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=3$\n- $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=3$" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280601 1113 #uuid "dce9cb91-9336-5a8f-9d65-459010bc2821" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280601 1145 "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function has a critical point (a corner) at $x=3.$ In this case,  ${f}^{′}(3)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement II is false. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ In this case,  the derivative is undefined at $x=3$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function does not have a local maximum at $x=3$ since $f(3)\\le f(x)$ for $x$ close enough  to the point $x=3,$ so,  actually,  $f(x)$ has local minimum at $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true." 13194139534176 true] [17592186280602 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280603 13194139534176 true] [17592186280602 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280604 13194139534176 true] [17592186280602 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280605 13194139534176 true] [17592186280602 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280606 13194139534176 true] [17592186280602 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280607 13194139534176 true] [17592186280602 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534176 true] [17592186280602 1041 "selection" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280602 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280605 13194139534176 true] [17592186280602 1110 #uuid "46661a1d-3864-5a96-a5e2-b5253f52d20d" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280603 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280603 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280603 1111 #uuid "ba56ec51-0bdd-5152-8945-39ae8bedd052" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280604 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280604 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280604 1111 #uuid "ef95798c-ff6e-534f-935a-e47b1e23d900" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280605 1034 "I only" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280605 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280605 1111 #uuid "65897179-1624-586f-83d7-cb1f04490553" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280606 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280606 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280606 1111 #uuid "3d5d1f4b-9b21-5ee4-8851-623ea3a7adda" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280607 1034 "II only" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280607 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280607 1111 #uuid "f83b5b45-6a2a-5f8c-9b5b-00c3448fa764" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280608 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280609 13194139534176 true] [17592186280608 1089 "q-48737" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280608 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534176 true] [17592186280608 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/4a2298a8255a86c803560793658593a22f7f77590ef350c1e0cebd974fce7750.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- $f(x)$ has  a stationary point at $x=0$\n- $f(x)$ has  a local maximum at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=2$" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280608 1113 #uuid "e1648c41-f8a3-54c4-a6c4-08a83701a800" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280608 1145 "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ In this case,  the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=0$ and therefore, ${f}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=2$ since $f(2)\\ge f(x)$ for all $x$ close enough to $x=2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of the function has a sharp edge (corner) at $x=2$ and therefore, ${f}^{′}(2)$ does not exist. Hence, $x=2$ is a critical point of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, all statements are true." 13194139534176 true] [17592186280609 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280610 13194139534176 true] [17592186280609 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280611 13194139534176 true] [17592186280609 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280612 13194139534176 true] [17592186280609 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280613 13194139534176 true] [17592186280609 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280614 13194139534176 true] [17592186280609 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534176 true] [17592186280609 1041 "selection" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280609 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280613 13194139534176 true] [17592186280609 1110 #uuid "6b13adb5-9271-58df-a814-55e4bc278f28" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280610 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280610 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280610 1111 #uuid "7bfacec1-1bdf-5550-9d6c-13eb465aaf8c" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280611 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280611 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280611 1111 #uuid "c0220f13-86a9-5209-ba9a-13e1418a7b22" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280612 1034 "III only" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280612 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280612 1111 #uuid "3ed100cd-2886-5b4c-8a2d-5cfbe8c75069" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280613 1034 "I, II and III" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280613 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280613 1111 #uuid "6ab56549-2e7c-5847-83cf-24e69d803d84" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280614 1034 "II only" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280614 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280614 1111 #uuid "b8c2b907-1524-5970-b8e8-e077011d69ae" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280615 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280616 13194139534176 true] [17592186280615 1089 "q-73399" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280615 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534176 true] [17592186280615 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/432991e403167bd560517072d24058c3f2106f093cdcad08f3b5f0ed486f9504.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. What are the $x$-coordinates of the critical points?" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280615 1113 #uuid "ece606bc-4b2a-5813-a3c1-ba2ac8790c10" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280615 1145 "The critical points of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ doesn't exist. Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal or where the tangent does not exist, respectively. \n\nIn the given graph, we can see that \n\n- at $x=3$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{′}(3)=0,$ while\n\n- at $x=7$ there is a discontinuity, so  ${f}^{′}(7)$ doesn't exist.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/e035d64e759108be78c54d0d8d1018906ef253bdcdaff548edd185b8b6543505.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, there are two critical points, and their $x$-coordinates are $x=3$ and $x=7.$" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280616 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280617 13194139534176 true] [17592186280616 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280618 13194139534176 true] [17592186280616 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280619 13194139534176 true] [17592186280616 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280620 13194139534176 true] [17592186280616 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280621 13194139534176 true] [17592186280616 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534176 true] [17592186280616 1041 "selection" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280616 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280620 13194139534176 true] [17592186280616 1110 #uuid "db7b9a07-56fe-5751-bb1b-51c088e24cdc" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280617 1034 "$x=7$ only" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280617 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280617 1111 #uuid "e01eed10-4f2d-50b4-ae2f-8468ea94f0de" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280618 1034 "$x=-1$  and $x=7$" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280618 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280618 1111 #uuid "a8b16167-95ec-5d45-a09a-860730a4ddff" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280619 1034 "$x=3$ only" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280619 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280619 1111 #uuid "d9e0e7e0-d0d2-5d00-be37-746b1796e279" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280620 1034 "$x=3$ and $x=7$" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280620 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280620 1111 #uuid "484d8f9b-d06a-5592-a30b-47258a4acfdc" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280621 1034 "$x=-1$ only" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280621 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280621 1111 #uuid "3bd1b7ee-b130-5178-809d-86ca37411752" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280622 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280623 13194139534176 true] [17592186280622 1089 "q-73409" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280622 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534176 true] [17592186280622 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/7c5bc75101a3bf4b4074bf60c1ab68962f2ed0de559f889365c3807be2fa833c.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above. What are the $x$-coordinates of all the critical points?" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280622 1113 #uuid "6fc62285-d6ad-563b-a202-c3fe4f3d9e71" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280622 1145 "The critical points of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ doesn't exist. Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal or where the tangent does not exist, respectively.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that\n\n- at any point between $x=2$ and $x=6$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ while\n\n- at $x=2$ and at $x=6$ there are sharp corners, hence ${f}^{′}(2)$ and ${f}^{′}(6)$ don't exist.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/6e4299d762e26ff3de85ae0fd766d28368de937098dfcf232ce9a234fee826e1.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, all numbers in the closed interval $[2,6]$ are critical points for $f(x).$" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280624 13194139534176 true] [17592186280623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280625 13194139534176 true] [17592186280623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280626 13194139534176 true] [17592186280623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280627 13194139534176 true] [17592186280623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280628 13194139534176 true] [17592186280623 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534176 true] [17592186280623 1041 "selection" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280623 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280628 13194139534176 true] [17592186280623 1110 #uuid "09e609df-9895-5b71-b35b-1a65a8539dcf" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280624 1034 "$x=6$ only" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280624 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280624 1111 #uuid "4c677eed-072e-5a9d-ba1e-38ed2a01a256" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280625 1034 "$x=2$ only" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280625 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280625 1111 #uuid "cd25040a-e948-5636-bdbe-1cc158adf2f3" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280626 1034 "None" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280626 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280626 1111 #uuid "6b655f12-b071-5d9e-89b0-51c0d15f6c03" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280627 1034 "$x=2$ and $x=6$ only" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280627 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280627 1111 #uuid "e4fb4479-a57e-50a2-8f0e-db3cffbe2cc2" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280628 1034 "All points in the closed interval $[2,6]$" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280628 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534176 true] [17592186280628 1111 #uuid "b1ed7f70-685a-5c90-960a-8487eb65e6b5" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280629 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280630 13194139534176 true] [17592186280629 1089 "q-73410" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280629 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534176 true] [17592186280629 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/eab84ed04b86678fb4a8d23c010e9a22beead062595b49818ac95e996c3173e6.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above. What are the $x$-coordinates of all the stationary points?" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280629 1113 #uuid "a831b1fe-cda9-5ab9-bf01-7acc19edee37" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280629 1145 "The stationary points of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that at any point between $x=3$ and $x=4$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at those points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/ab200bebf80bbd1abe6e0c43ec124fe359da25f38d4346d25d8741decc88aa26.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinates of the stationary points of $f(x)$, are all values in the open interval $(3,4).$ \n\n**Note:** At $x=3$ and $x=4$, there are sharp corners, so ${f}^{′}(x)$ doesn't exist at those points. This gives us two more critical points. But they are not stationary points." 13194139534176 true] [17592186280630 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280631 13194139534176 true] [17592186280630 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280632 13194139534176 true] [17592186280630 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280633 13194139534176 true] [17592186280630 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280634 13194139534176 true] [17592186280630 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280635 13194139534176 true] [17592186280630 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534176 true] [17592186280630 1041 "selection" 13194139534176 true] [17592186280630 1104 #edb/ref 175